{"document":1,"title":"user in #cf22b7bf:answer_ae3a122b_2: I'm looking for some new workout playlists to try...","updated_at":"2023-06-21T05:48:00+00:00","source_type":"slack","content":"That's a lot of options! I'll check out a few of those playlists, thanks. By the way, speaking of my cardio days, I've been doing great and I just realized I've lost 10 pounds since I started going consistently to the gym 3 months ago. Do you have any tips on how to keep my energy levels up during my workouts?","metadata":"{\"Channel\": \"cf22b7bf:answer_ae3a122b_2\"}"}
{"document":2,"title":"user in #cf22b7bf:answer_ae3a122b_1: I'm trying to plan out my workout routine for the...","updated_at":"2023-05-21T12:38:00+00:00","source_type":"slack","content":"3. **Training time**: How much time can you dedicate to training each week? 4. **Summer timeline**: When exactly do you want to run the 10K, and how many weeks do you have to train? Assuming you're a beginner or intermediate runner, a general rule of thumb is to increase your weekly mileage by no more than 10% each week to allow for adaptation and reduce the risk of injury. If you're new to running, it's recommended to start with shorter distances (3-5K) and gradually build up to longer distances. A 10K might be a challenging goal, but it's achievable with consistent training and dedication. Here's a rough estimate of a 12-week training plan to help you reach your goal: * Weeks 1-4: Build up to running 3-4 times a week, with one longer run (5-6K) on the weekends. * Weeks 5-8: Increase your weekly mileage by 10% each week, with one longer run (7-8K) on the weekends. * Weeks 9-12: Continue to increase your weekly mileage, with one longer run (9-10K) on the weekends. Remember to also incorporate rest days, cross-training, and proper nutrition to support your training. I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any more questions or need further guidance.\nI like the exercises you suggested, especially the Pallof Press and Bird Dog. I'll definitely add those to my routine. As for my 10K goal, I'm a complete beginner when it comes to running, but I've been doing a lot of cardio at the gym and I've been trying to incorporate more outdoor activities. I've been doing 30-minute walks during my lunch break, 3 times a week. I think I can dedicate about 4-5 hours a week to training. Can you help me create a more detailed training plan with specific workouts and distances? Also, by the way, I've been tracking my progress using a fitness app, and I've burned an average of 2500 calories per week, which is higher than my goal. I've also lost about 5 pounds in the past month, which is slow progress, but I'm okay with that.","metadata":"{\"Channel\": \"cf22b7bf:answer_ae3a122b_1\"}"}
{"document":2,"title":"user in #cf22b7bf:answer_ae3a122b_1: I'm trying to plan out my workout routine for the...","updated_at":"2023-05-21T12:38:00+00:00","source_type":"slack","content":"I think I'll try to find a running buddy through online platforms or local running groups. That way, I can find someone with a similar fitness level and goal. And thanks for the hydration tips! I'll make sure to drink plenty of water before and during my runs.\n\nBy the way, I was thinking about my progress so far, and I realized that I've been doing pretty well with my workouts and diet. I've lost 5 pounds in the past month, which is slow but steady progress. I'm hoping to continue this trend and reach my goal weight by the end of the summer. Do you have any tips on how to stay motivated and focused on my long-term goals?","metadata":"{\"Channel\": \"cf22b7bf:answer_ae3a122b_1\"}"}
